WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:06.400
Vsauce, Kevin here, and I’ve combined one
of the deadliest games anyone can play with

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:13.520
one of the stupidest -- which is why I will
need two complete morons to play it:

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:16.560
Thomas Edison and Nikola Tesla!

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:22.720
Okay, they’re actually two of the most legendary
geniuses of all time, but their rivalry was

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:26.320
SERIOUS -- especially over&nbsp;
Tesla’s alternating-current&nbsp;

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:29.200
AC electricity and Edison’s direct-current

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:35.120
DC, which we now call The War of the Currents.
Throw in decades of personal disputes and

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:42.080
they’ve got a score to settle -- their balloon
avatars will fight to the death with the help

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:46.240
of a toy revolver and a single needle-dart.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:52.160
They’ll be playing a form of Russian Roulette
-- watch my ‘Surviving the Deadliest Game’

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:58.720
video for the math on the original. And the
stupidest game humans have played is probably

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:06.640
the classic duel where two people just stand
there and shoot at each other. En garde! That’s..

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:09.760
That’s fencing but… you know what I mean.

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:16.880
These two titans of the electron are gonna
duel this one gun Russian Roulette style.

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:17.520
Okay?

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:23.920
The game is simple: Player A loads the cartridge,
spins the cylinder, and shoots at Player B.

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:31.840
He’s so close that it’s impossible to
miss. The gun will either fire or… not.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:38.640
If it doesn’t fire, he hands the gun to
Player B, who then spins the cylinder again

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:46.080
and then shoots at Player A. Then eventually
it goes on until one of them is crowned the

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.160
Emperor of Electricity.

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:54.640
First, DON’T TRY THIS ACTUAL GAME PLEASE.
Especially because I’ll show you exactly

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:59.920
how to try this actual game without trying
this actual game.

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:06.480
Anyway, for now, what we really want to know
is who’s more likely to win, Edison or Tesla.

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:12.240
Someone’s gotta go first, so I’ll use
an unbiased coin flip to determine the order.

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:21.840
Heads for Edison, Tails for Tesla. Here we
go… Heads for Edison.

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:23.280
Headison.

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:28.480
All right… here we go first shot for Edison
to Tesla…Wait.

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:36.640
To figure out whether there’s an advantage
to going first, we can just map out the scenarios

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:42.800
in which Edison wins -- we don’t even need
numbers. SORRY, NUMBERS. Let’s use an E

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:48.480
when the gun fires for Edison and an x for
when the gun does NOT fire. Here’s a map

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:51.600
of all the possible Edison victories:

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:58.880
This goes on until the gun fires. E’s can
only fall on the odd rounds because Edison

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:04.240
went first. And that’s really the difference
here -- Edison and Tesla have the same chance

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:10.640
of winning every round -- 1 out of 6 -- but
Edison can win on the very first round before

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:13.120
Tesla even gets a turn.

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:20.880
That’s a major advantage, but how much?
And how has this first round advantage caused

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:26.160
one of the biggest games in the world to literally
change its rule!? We’ll get to that later…

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:35.120
For now it’s time for Edison’s first shot…
Let me re-spin the old revolver here. Here

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:41.440
we go. Tesla survives!

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:45.360
Let’s think about this...

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:51.120
The probability of Edison firing the gun is
⅙ from the first round plus the probability

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:57.520
of success in subsequent rounds -- and this
goes on and on as long as Edison and Tesla

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:03.120
keep missing. SPOILER: the total probability
of the gun firing for Edison comes out to

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:13.920
6/11, or .5454 repeating. So Edison’s got
a 54.5% chance of winning this duel, which

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:23.200
means Tesla… Tesla is at just 45.5% -- that’s
a 9% disadvantage for Tesla. Even in THIS

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:28.960
balloon rivalry the deck is stacked for Edison.

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:33.440
You can calculate manually how many trigger
pulls are in the average game of Idiot’s

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:39.760
Russian Roulette, but Paul J. Nahin’s Monte
Carlo simulations of 10,000 trials confirmed

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:46.160
that it’s about 6. Here’s what’s interesting:
about ⅔ of those games are over in the first

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:52.720
6 rounds. There’s a long tail with some
games going 20 or 30 rounds, but it’s generally

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:57.600
a quick game, so Edison getting the first
shot really matters.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:03.040
WAIT -- YOU can actually run Russian Roulette
simulations yourself without a computer program

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:08.880
or… anything more dangerous. You’ve got
a random number generator in your pocket.

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:17.680
Open the stopwatch, divide a second into sixths
-- 0.01 to 0.17 corresponds to the gun firing.

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:25.520
0.18 to 0.00 means it doesn’t fire. Start
the stopwatch, close your eyes, and stop it

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:32.640
-- the decimal value of the seconds is a random
firing simulator you can use to map out Russian

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:34.720
Roulette safely.

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:41.520
But now it’s time to get back to our game.
And it’s time for Tesla’s revenge….

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:53.200
Hold on. If Tesla going second means he’s
at such a disadvantage... how can we make

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:59.600
the game more fair? We can’t just play it
1,000 times alternating who goes first so

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:07.760
advantages even out. Someone’s gettin’
popped! We get one shot at this -- so let’s

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:12.720
just change the rules to compensate for giving
Edison the first shot.

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:20.080
NEW GAME: If Edison’s gun doesn’t fire
in Round 1, Tesla gets TWO shots. If neither

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:26.160
of Tesla’s shots fire, Edison gets THREE
shots -- this goes on until the gun fires.

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:32.080
Does that actually make the game more fair?
Is giving Tesla an additional shot after Round

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:36.000
1 enough to make up for his disadvantage?

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:36.960
NEW MAP:

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:40.400
Okay there’s the map of our new version
of the game.

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:45.280
And we can do the same sort of calculation
as before, but the probability of Edison’s

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:53.920
gun firing is now that . . . with an exponent
corresponding to each of Edison’s rounds.

00:06:53.920 --> 00:06:59.440
The alternating expanding rounds model reduces
Edison’s chance of winning all the way down

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:07.760
to… about 52.4%. That’s 2 points lower
for Edison and 2 points higher for ol’ Tesla

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:10.800
here, who’s now sitting at 47.6%.

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:15.760
It’s better. It’s not great, but it’s
better.

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:21.520
We can keep tweaking the rules. We can keep
running the math. But the most critical part

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:28.400
of the deadliest, dumbest game might not be
how it’s played -- but it’s actually that

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:31.840
coin flip that determines who goes first.

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:35.920
Which is what the National Football League,
one of the most popular sports leagues in

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:41.280
the world realized when it changed its sudden
death overtime rules in 2010. The team who

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:46.560
got the ball first won the game so often that
not winning the coin toss to determine who

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:53.360
played offense first meant you’d lose the
game nearly 3 out of 5 times.

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:58.080
But check this out. Look what I found. You’re
not gonna believe this. And if you do.. I

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:02.400
won’t believe you… Alright look. When
the NFL used sudden death overtime rules,

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:11.440
coin flip winners won 52.0% of the time, coin
flip losers won 43.4% of the time… the other

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:21.440
4.6% were ties. If we evenly-distributed those
ties as wins, that’s 54.3% to the coin flip

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:30.240
winner and 45.7% for the loser. SOUND FAMILIAR?
Those are almost the EXACT SAME odds of our

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:32.720
original Edison/Tesla duel game.

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:38.240
Maybe that’s why they called this… sudden
death.

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:43.600
Alright, let’s settle this duel once and
for all. Here we go. Okay so using our new

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:49.040
game rules, Tesla actually gets another shot.
Alright, shot number two for Tesla. Here we

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:53.840
go.

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:56.160
Feel lucky, Edison?

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:05.920
Oh! Edison survives. Now it goes back to Edison’s
turn. That’s two misses for Tesla. Here

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:12.080
we go. Now Edison gets three shots. Say your
prayers, Tesla. You’re gonna need em.

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:22.560
Oh! Survives once again. Okay. Two more shots
for Edison. Here we go.

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:28.800
Oh! Survives another one!

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:35.840
Alright, one more for Edison and then it’s
going back to Tesla. Here we are.

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:46.720
Oh! He made it through round two! Now Tesla
gets four tries!

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:53.040
Edison, you really should’ve done it that
time. Four shots for Tesla. Shot number one.

00:09:55.560 --> 00:09:59.040
Oh! Shot number one. Survived!

00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:03.840
Shot number two.

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:07.920
Ah! Shot number two! This game.. Maybe we’ll
make it to 30 rounds.

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:12.720
Here we go, spinning. Shot number three for
Tesla.

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:19.200
Oh! Shot number three! The game continues!

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:25.520
Shot number four. Chance number four. You’d
think one of these four would be it. Let’s

00:10:25.520 --> 00:10:26.020
find out.

00:10:29.120 --> 00:10:33.760
I can’t believe it! I can’t believe it!
Four down.

00:10:33.760 --> 00:10:40.560
Now it goes to five shots for Edison. Shot
number one for Edison.

00:10:44.720 --> 00:10:48.080
Alright. The game continues. The game continues.

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:57.760
Shot number two. Jeez! You’d think it..
One of these it would be it. Not so. Not so.

00:10:57.760 --> 00:11:01.840
I’m totally randomizing this, folks. No
no funny games here.

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:03.440
Shot number three.

00:11:08.920 --> 00:11:09.920
Oh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:19.440
Tesla! No! No! I’m so sorry! I am sorry
you have been reduced to this. This is all

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:27.520
that’s left of Nikola Tesla. Just a dressed-up
Pringles can. Is all we have left. Well. There

00:11:27.520 --> 00:11:35.120
ya go. Edison wins. The numbers worked. I’ve
got nothing more to say. Other than...

00:11:35.120 --> 00:11:41.840
And as always… thanks for watching.

